 "transcript": {
  "answer": "No. At **¥109.67 per A$1 on 16 October 2026** (latest RBA 4pm reading), the Aussie dollar is strong against the yen. It has slipped from recent highs, though.\n\n**Compared with the last few years: still high**\n| Period | AUD/JPY |\n|---|---|\n| Low, April 2025 (month-end) | 91.43 |\n| End June 2024 (2024 peak) | 106.61 |\n| End December 2025 | 104.83 |\n| End August 2026 (recent peak) | 114.54 |\n| **16 October 2026** | **109.67** |\n\n- The dollar buys about **20% more yen** than at the April 2025 low.\n- The current rate is above any month-end level from 2023 to 2025.\n- The RBA data available to me starts in 2023. From general knowledge rather than this dataset, ¥110 is also high by longer-term standards, above the peaks of roughly ¥105–107 seen in 2007 and 2013.\n\n**Recently: it has eased**\n- It's down about **4.3%** from ¥114.54 at end-August and ¥114.63 on 1 September.\n- Since late September it has held at around ¥109.4–110.7 rather than kept falling.\n\n**It's not just the yen**\n- AUD/USD: **0.6947** in October, down from 0.7166 in August, but well above the low-0.62s of early 2025.\n- Trade-weighted index: **64.4**, down from 66.6 in May 2026, but above the 59–61 range of 2024–25.\n\nSo the recent dip is a broad pullback in the Aussie dollar, and the yen hasn't strengthened in particular.\n\n**Bottom line:** For anything priced in yen, like a Japan trip or Japanese imports, the A$ still buys a lot. It just buys a bit less than it did at its August–September peak.",
